import{_ as t,o as i,c as s,ah as a}from"./chunks/framework.ojwJKwrc.js";const u=JSON.parse('{"title":"Taxi / cocotb comparison","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"benchmarks/taxi.md","filePath":"benchmarks/taxi.md"}'),r={name:"benchmarks/taxi.md"};function n(o,e,l,h,d,c){return i(),s("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[a(`<h1 id="taxi-cocotb-comparison" tabindex="-1">Taxi / cocotb comparison <a class="header-anchor" href="#taxi-cocotb-comparison" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Taxi / cocotb comparison&quot;">​</a></h1><p>The <strong>Taxi / cocotb</strong> tab on the <a href="./">benchmark dashboard</a> compares Celox with Verilator using unmodified, self-checking upstream SystemVerilog designs and Python testbenches. The <code>Taxi Benchmark</code> workflow runs daily at 03:17 UTC (12:17 JST) and supports manual dispatch. Only a successful scheduled or manual <code>master</code> run publishes history.</p><p>The <a href="https://github.com/celox-sim/celox/blob/master/benchmarks/taxi/manifest.json" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">manifest</a> pins <a href="https://github.com/fpganinja/taxi" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">fpganinja/taxi</a> to <code>cc70b270b910d369ab1ad7b3855e76399fd461f1</code>, including sources, tops, parameters, expected test counts and random seed 12345. The Python requirements are pinned; CI uses Ubuntu 26.04, Python 3.13, cocotb 2.1.0 and Verilator 5.052. The local setup action builds Verilator from the fixed upstream commit <code>ea338be98e1e838d3518809ce8899f85a009963c</code> on the runner. Its installation cache is separated by Ubuntu version, architecture, compiler, libc and build recipe. Cache hits still verify the installed version and source identity. Each configuration has a six-hour job limit and a one-hour limit per compiler or simulator process; RX configurations run the complete test matrix three times per simulator.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Configuration</th><th>Design</th><th style="text-align:right;">Tests per simulator per sample</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>lfsr64</td><td>64-bit self-synchronous LFSR descrambler</td><td style="text-align:right;">1</td></tr><tr><td>phy64</td><td>64-bit 10G Ethernet PHY</td><td style="text-align:right;">3</td></tr><tr><td>rx16</td><td>16-bit BASE-X receive, gearbox/SGMII/PTP enabled</td><td style="text-align:right;">78</td></tr><tr><td>rx8</td><td>8-bit BASE-X receive, gearbox/SGMII/PTP enabled</td><td style="text-align:right;">78</td></tr><tr><td>rx32</td><td>32-bit BASE-R receive, gearbox/USXGMII/PTP enabled</td><td style="text-align:right;">312</td></tr></tbody></table><p>These are five fixed configurations, totaling <strong>472 tests per simulator per sample</strong>, rather than the entire Taxi project&#39;s parameter matrix. Each daily run executes three samples of each configuration for each simulator.</p><h2 id="measurement" tabindex="-1">Measurement <a class="header-anchor" href="#measurement" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Measurement&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Each configuration gets one VM. Both engines compile and run sequentially on the same logical CPU, alternating Celox/Verilator then Verilator/Celox. The third pair runs in the first order again. Different configurations can use different CPU models. CPU model, runner, boot ID, tool versions, CLI binary hash, source hashes, parameters, commands, logs, XML and every raw timing are retained in the <code>taxi-*</code> artifacts for 30 days. The dashboard separates CPU models.</p><p>The charts show three distinct measurements:</p><ul><li><strong>Test body:</strong> median of three sums of cocotb XML test times. This includes Python stimulus, resets, simulator interaction and assertions. Cocotb 2.1.0 uses <code>time.monotonic()</code> for its test durations. A shared GPI startup check verifies the pinned version without replacing its clock, measurement boundaries or XML writer. Wall-clock corrections cannot corrupt elapsed times. It measures the usable cocotb workload, including Python overhead.</li><li><strong>Whole process:</strong> median of three fresh executable processes, from spawn to exit. Celox&#39;s Python discovery and result checks are inside this interval; Verilator&#39;s Python environment is configured beforehand. Both retain Python initialization and simulator startup. Compiling the RTL is excluded.</li><li><strong>HDL compilation:</strong> one fresh build per engine, before any runtime samples. Celox includes frontend analysis, native code generation and attached executable writing. Verilator includes RTL-to-C++ generation and serial C++ compilation. C++ compiler caching is disabled. Building Celox itself and installing tools are excluded.</li></ul><p>Celox uses native O1 and two-state execution. Verilator uses cocotb&#39;s standard <code>--vpi --public-flat-rw</code> adapter and its default C++ <code>-Os</code> setting. Tracing is off for both. RX32 explicitly uses <code>1ns/1fs</code> in both builds. Diagnostic relaxations are explicit in the runner. Both simulators receive the same unchanged source bytes and parameter values.</p><p>Every sample must contain the expected number of unique, passing tests. Skips, missing XML, duplicate identities, invalid timings, nonzero exit, timeout, or unequal per-test simulated durations fail the run. Any failed configuration prevents the daily set from publishing. A failed run cannot add zero or partial performance points. Prior successful history remains visible with its original date. The raw report also records the median of paired Celox/Verilator body-time ratios; values below one favor Celox. This differs from dividing the two medians. Shared CI machines introduce noise; repeat small differences before drawing performance conclusions.</p><h2 id="run-locally" tabindex="-1">Run locally <a class="header-anchor" href="#run-locally" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Run locally&quot;">​</a></h2><div class="language-bash vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">bash</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">python3.13</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> -m</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> venv</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> work/taxi-venv</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">work/taxi-venv/bin/pip</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> install</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> -r</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> benchmarks/taxi/requirements.txt</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">cargo</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> build</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> --locked</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> --release</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> -p</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> celox-vpi</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> --features</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> systemverilog</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> --bin</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> celox</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">work/taxi-venv/bin/python</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> scripts/run-taxi-bench.py</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> prepare</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> work/taxi-source</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">work/taxi-venv/bin/python</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> scripts/run-taxi-bench.py</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> run</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> phy64</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> work/taxi-source</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> target/release/celox</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> work/taxi-phy64</span></span></code></pre></div><p>Install Verilator 5.052 and a C++ compiler first. Choose a fresh output directory for every run; existing results are never reused as a new measurement. Use <code>--samples 1</code> for local integration checks; daily publication requires all three samples. Results from a locally modified checkout are diagnostics, not published benchmark evidence.</p>`,14)])])}const m=t(r,[["render",n]]);export{u as __pageData,m as default};
